(in-ns 'go.runtime.metrics) (go/file "description.go" :imports [

    ^{:line 7} [godebugs "internal/godebugs"]])


(go/type Description "Description describes a runtime metric.\n" (struct























    ^{:line 34 :tag string :doc "Name is the full name of the metric which includes the unit.\n\nThe format of the metric may be described by the following regular expression.\n\n\t^(?P<name>/[^:]+):(?P<unit>[^:*/]+(?:[*/][^:*/]+)*)$\n\nThe format splits the name into two components, separated by a colon: a path which always\nstarts with a /, and a machine-parseable unit. The name may contain any valid Unicode\ncodepoint in between / characters, but by convention will try to stick to lowercase\ncharacters and hyphens. An example of such a path might be \"/memory/heap/free\".\n\nThe unit is by convention a series of lowercase English unit names (singular or plural)\nwithout prefixes delimited by '*' or '/'. The unit names may contain any valid Unicode\ncodepoint that is not a delimiter.\nExamples of units might be \"seconds\", \"bytes\", \"bytes/second\", \"cpu-seconds\",\n\"byte*cpu-seconds\", and \"bytes/second/second\".\n\nFor histograms, multiple units may apply. For instance, the units of the buckets and\nthe count. By convention, for histograms, the units of the count are always \"samples\"\nwith the type of sample evident by the metric's name, while the unit in the name\nspecifies the buckets' unit.\n\nA complete name might look like \"/memory/heap/free:bytes\".\n"} Name


    ^{:line 37 :tag string :doc "Description is an English language sentence describing the metric.\n"} Description





    ^{:line 43 :tag ValueKind :doc "Kind is the kind of value for this metric.\n\nThe purpose of this field is to allow users to filter out metrics whose values are\ntypes which their application may not understand.\n"} Kind






    ^{:line 50 :tag bool :doc "Cumulative is whether or not the metric is cumulative. If a cumulative metric is just\na single number, then it increases monotonically. If the metric is a distribution,\nthen each bucket count increases monotonically.\n\nThis flag thus indicates whether or not it's useful to compute a rate from this value.\n"} Cumulative))




